WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Edge protection moves defensive capacity closer to the internet and farther away from

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.500
fragile origin systems.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.440
A CDN can cache content and absorb large volumes of ordinary web requests.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.020
A WAF can enforce application-layer rules at the edge.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.760
DNS protection can keep name resolution available during pressure.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.500
Anycast routing can distribute traffic across many locations.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.440
A scrubbing provider can help filter traffic before it reaches the organization or cloud

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.620
environment.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.720
These services do not remove the need for engineering,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.660
but they can change the scale and location of the problem.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.440
The most important edge design principle is that the origin should not be unnecessarily

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.620
exposed.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.560
If a service is meant to be protected by a CDN, WAF, or provider front door,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.780
direct paths to the origin should be understood and restricted where possible.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:50.960
Otherwise,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.300
an incident can bypass the edge and put pressure directly on the systems least able to

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.480
handle it.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.540
This is also why DNS records, certificates, provider routing,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.040
and emergency change procedures must be documented before an event.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.380
Provider coordination is not optional during large events.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.680
Internal teams may not have visibility or control over upstream capacity,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.420
regional routing, edge filtering, or scrubbing policies.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.360
Before an incident, the organization should know which providers protect which services,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.940
what support level is available, how to open emergency tickets,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.600
which contacts are authorized, what evidence providers need,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.380
and what controls can be changed quickly.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:35.040
A phone number hidden in a purchasing record is not a response plan.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:40.140
Emergency edge changes should be treated like operational changes, not improvisation.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.080
The team should know the intended effect, the risk, the rollback, the owner,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.300
and the monitoring signal.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.040
Controls should be tested in approved environments or low-risk windows,

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:54.860
using authorized load testing with clear scope and safe rates.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.680
During a real event, document every provider action, routing change, rule change,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.340
and business decision.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.840
That timeline is essential for recovery and improvement.
